        <div id="cocoa" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            7. Is the Cocoa in Chocolate Whey Dutch-Processed or Natural?
          </h2>

          <p>
            Most chocolate whey uses Dutch-processed cocoa, which labels list as "cocoa processed with alkali" or "alkalized cocoa". Gold Standard, ISO100, Thorne and Hydrowhey all list alkalized cocoa, per their labels at the time of writing. Transparent Labs lists plain "cocoa powder" without saying whether it is alkalized, and Nitro-Tech's ingredient list on MuscleTech's site lists cocoa.
          </p>

          <p>
            Alkalizing treats cocoa with an alkaline salt to raise its pH. It darkens the color, softens the sharp, sour notes of natural cocoa and helps the powder disperse in liquid, which is why protein brands like it: a Dutch-processed chocolate shake looks richer and mixes more smoothly. Natural cocoa tastes more acidic and fruity, and some buyers prefer it for that.
          </p>

          <p>
            The trade-off is flavanols, the plant compounds behind most of cocoa's health claims. A 2008 study in the Journal of Agricultural and Food Chemistry found that alkalization cuts cocoa's flavanol content, and heavily alkalized powders lost most of it. In practice this matters less than it sounds. A protein scoop contains only a few grams of cocoa at most, so even natural cocoa in a shake would supply a small fraction of what flavanol studies use. Buy chocolate whey for the protein, not for cocoa's antioxidants.
          </p>

          <p>
            How much cocoa is actually in the scoop? Most brands do not say, because ingredients are listed by weight without amounts. Transparent Labs is the exception: it prints 960 mg of cocoa powder per serving, under 1 g. Other brands likely use more, judging by where cocoa sits in their ingredient lists, often right after the protein. More cocoa usually means a darker, more bitter-chocolate taste, more carbohydrate and fiber, and, as the next section explains, more of the trace metals cocoa naturally carries.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">What else is in a chocolate scoop</h3>

          <p>
            Beyond cocoa, expect lecithin to help mixing, natural and artificial flavors, salt, gums for thickness, and a sweetener. Nitro-Tech adds an enzyme blend and carrageenan, Hydrowhey adds a creamer of sunflower oil and maltodextrin, and Thorne adds bromelain and papain. None of these is a safety concern at normal intakes, but shorter lists are easier on people who react to gums or sugar alcohols.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="heavy-metals" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            8. Does Chocolate Whey Have More Lead and Cadmium Than Vanilla?
          </h2>

          <p>
            Yes, on average: chocolate protein powders test higher for lead and cadmium than vanilla, because cocoa itself carries both metals. The Clean Label Project's January 2025 report on 160 protein products found chocolate powders had about four times more lead than vanilla. Whey-based chocolate powders still tend to test far lower than plant-based ones, so a chocolate whey is a middle-ground choice.
          </p>

          <p>
            The metals arrive in different ways. Cadmium is taken up from the soil the cocoa tree grows in, so levels depend partly on where the beans are farmed, and the EU sets a legal maximum for cadmium in cocoa powder sold to consumers. Lead is thought to land on the beans mostly after harvest, from soil and dust during fermenting and drying. Neither is something a protein brand can fully remove, but it can choose suppliers and test lots.
          </p>

          <p>
            Some chocolate wheys have tested well. In Consumer Reports' October 2025 investigation, Gold Standard 100% Whey in chocolate measured about 56% of CR's daily lead level of concern per serving, and CR placed it among its better choices for regular use. CR's level of concern is 0.5 micrograms of lead a day, taken from California's Prop 65 safe-harbor level, which is far stricter than federal limits. Our guide to the <a href="/best-protein-powder-without-heavy-metals/">best protein powder without heavy metals</a> lists the published results in full.
          </p>

          <p>
            Most of the other powders here have no published, product-specific lead figure that we could find, which is not the same as a bad result. Transparent Labs publishes certificates of analysis, and NSF certification includes contaminant limits, so both give some assurance. If metals worry you, the practical steps are simple: choose a whey over a plant powder, keep to one or two chocolate scoops a day, rotate in vanilla or unflavored, and favor brands that publish test results.
          </p>

          <p>
            Pregnant women, people trying to conceive and parents buying for teenagers have the most reason to be careful, because lead affects developing brains and there is no known safe level. If you fall into one of those groups, or have kidney disease, ask a doctor or registered dietitian whether a daily protein powder is the right choice and how much is sensible.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="sweeteners" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            9. Which Chocolate Whey Protein Powders Have No Artificial Sweeteners?
          </h2>

          <p>
            Two chocolate wheys on this page have no artificial sweeteners: Transparent Labs Milk Chocolate, sweetened only with stevia, and Thorne Whey Isolate Chocolate, sweetened with a little cane sugar and stevia (rebaudioside A), per their labels at the time of writing. The other four use sucralose, acesulfame potassium or both.
          </p>

          <p>
            Chocolate is the flavor where sweeteners do the most work. Cocoa is bitter, so a chocolate shake needs more sweetness than a vanilla one to taste like milk chocolate rather than baking cocoa. That is why many chocolate wheys combine two sweeteners: sucralose for the main sweetness and acesulfame potassium to round off its aftertaste. Gold Standard's Double Rich Chocolate panel we checked lists acesulfame potassium alone, while ISO100 pairs sucralose with stevia.
          </p>

          <p>
            Whether to avoid artificial sweeteners is a personal call. Sucralose and acesulfame potassium are approved by the FDA, and the amounts in a protein scoop are small. Some people avoid them because of taste, digestive discomfort or a preference for fewer additives, and research on long-term effects of sweeteners on gut bacteria and appetite is still developing. If they do not bother you, they let a chocolate scoop stay at 1 to 2 g of sugar.
          </p>

          <p>
            Real-sugar chocolate wheys are the other route. Naked Nutrition's chocolate whey uses organic coconut sugar and cocoa with no sweetener system at all, but per its Amazon listing that means 5 g of sugar and 160 calories in a two-scoop serving. Our <a href="/naked-nutrition-protein-powder-review/">Naked Nutrition review</a> covers it in detail. Stevia and monk fruit sit in between: no sugar and no artificial sweetener, but some buyers notice a bitter or lingering finish, which cocoa can either mask or amplify.
          </p>

