        <div id="calories" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            10. Why Do Chocolate Whey Scoops Have More Calories and Carbs Than Vanilla?
          </h2>

          <p>
            Chocolate whey scoops are usually bigger and carry more calories and carbohydrate than vanilla because cocoa takes up room in the scoop without adding much protein. To keep the protein figure the same, brands make the chocolate scoop heavier, and the extra grams bring calories, carbs and fiber with them. The gap is usually 5 to 40 calories a scoop.
          </p>

          <p>
            Two labels show it clearly. MuscleTech's Nitro-Tech gives 30 g of protein in both flavors, but the Milk Chocolate scoop is 46 g and 160 calories while Vanilla Cream is 45 g and 150 calories, per MuscleTech's labels at the time of writing. Naked Nutrition's unflavored whey gives 25 g of protein in a 30 g scoop with 120 calories, per Naked's site, while its chocolate version needs two scoops and 160 calories for the same 25 g, per the Amazon listing, because cocoa and coconut sugar fill the difference.
          </p>

          <p>
            Cocoa powder itself is not light. USDA figures for unsweetened cocoa powder work out to roughly 2.3 calories, 0.6 g of carbohydrate and 0.4 g of fiber per gram, with some fat and a little protein. Five grams of cocoa therefore adds about 11 calories and 3 g of carbohydrate before any sugar or flavoring. Add a gum, a creamer or real sugar, as some chocolate tubs do, and the gap grows.
          </p>

          <p>
            For most people the difference is trivial: 10 to 40 calories is a bite of fruit. It matters if you are cutting hard, tracking carbs for a ketogenic diet, or comparing protein density between brands. A useful check is protein per 100 calories. Thorne's chocolate isolate gives 21 g per 100 calories, Transparent Labs about 21.5 g, ISO100 and Hydrowhey about 21 g, Gold Standard 20 g and Nitro-Tech under 19 g.
          </p>

          <p>
            Always compare the chocolate panel with the chocolate panel. A brand's headline "120 calories" is often the vanilla or unflavored figure, and the chocolate tub you buy may differ by a few grams of scoop and a few grams of carbohydrate. If you track macros closely, weigh your scoop once with a kitchen scale; plastic scoops often hold more or less than the label weight.
          </p>
        </div>

        <!-- Common Mistakes -->
        <div id="mistakes" class="scroll-mt-24 mt-16">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mb-6">
            11. Common Mistakes to Avoid
          </h2>

          <div class="space-y-6 my-8">
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Comparing the vanilla label with a chocolate tub</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Brands often quote calories and carbs from their vanilla or unflavored version. Chocolate scoops are usually heavier, with more carbohydrate from cocoa. Before you choose on macros, open the chocolate flavor's own nutrition panel on the brand site or Amazon listing, not the headline figures in the product title.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Judging value by tub price</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                A $55 tub and a $65 tub can differ wildly in cost per gram. Nitro-Tech's 4 lb tub held 48 portions of 25 g at $55.67, while Thorne's tub held about 25 portions at $65. Divide the price by servings times protein over 25 to get a fair comparison before you buy.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Assuming "tested" means certified for sport</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Brand pages say "lab tested" or "banned-substance tested" freely. Only a registry listing at nsfsport.com or Informed Sport covers drug-tested athletes. Gold Standard Double Rich Chocolate and Thorne Chocolate are in the NSF registry, but always search the registry for the exact flavor and tub size, and keep your lot number.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Drinking several chocolate scoops a day</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Cocoa carries lead and cadmium, and chocolate powders tested higher than vanilla in the Clean Label Project's 2025 report. One chocolate scoop of whey is a modest exposure. Three a day adds up. Rotate in a vanilla or unflavored whey if protein powder supplies a large share of your protein.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Buying a 5 lb tub of an untried flavor</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Chocolate wheys taste very different, from sweet milk chocolate to bitter cocoa with stevia. Buyers who dislike a flavor rarely finish the tub. Start with a 2 lb tub, a 1.4 lb ISO100 or Thorne's single-serve packets, then move to the largest size once you know what you like.
              </p>
            </div>
          </div>
        </div>

